\DOC X86_GEN_ACCSTEPS_TAC

\TYPE {X86_GEN_ACCSTEPS_TAC : (string -> tactic) -> thm * (thm option array) -> int list -> int list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes a block of x86 machine instructions inside an
{eventually x86} goal, advancing the goal's current state variable and
recording the effect of each executed instruction as new assumptions, with
accumulator-style arithmetic steps summarised as numeric carry/product
equations.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are: {acc_preproc : string -> tactic}, a tactic generator applied
during processing of each accumulated step (in the observed traces either a
no-op or a pair of {RULE_ASSUM_TAC} rewrites normalising word
subtraction/multiplication forms in the assumptions);
{th : thm * (thm option array)}, the execution/code-length record for the
program being stepped (its length theorem is displayed alongside the goal);
{anums : int list}, the step numbers to be treated as accumulation steps,
yielding arithmetic equations; and {snums : int list}, the step numbers to be
executed symbolically. The conclusion's {eventually x86 (\s'. ...)} body is
left unchanged, while its final state argument becomes the new state variable
reached after the requested steps. Among the assumptions, those describing the
old state (code loaded, register and memory reads, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation, and any initial trivial {MAYCHANGE [] s s}) are discarded and
replaced by the corresponding hypotheses about the new state:
register/flag/memory read equations, the updated {MAYCHANGE} chain from the
initial state, and {bytes_loaded} for the new state. For each accumulation step
an arithmetic equation over {&}/{val}/{bitval} (addition, subtraction or
multiplication with carry) is added. Some purely arithmetic or side assumptions
are dropped and re-added unchanged, so they may change position in the
assumption list.
{
               A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s
       ================================================  X86_GEN_ACCSTEPS_TAC acc_preproc th anums snums
              A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s'
}
\noindent where {A'} replaces the state assumptions of {A} by ones about the
new state, plus the arithmetic step equations.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour on goals
whose conclusion is an {eventually x86} statement.

\ENDDOC
